From ed822ce7fc073890ad008cfbe91ef42c17f904e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shaun Adams <s.adams@f5.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 17:16:48 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 05/42] configstore: extend tenant_id to providers, keys, MCP
 clients
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 1 schema rollout step 3 — the user-config tables that each
tenant "brings their own of" in a SaaS deployment:
  - config_providers
  - config_keys
  - config_mcp_clients

Same shape as patches 0007 and 0008: AddColumn tenant_id (default
"default") + idempotent backfill. The struct tag uses
`gorm:"...;index;default:default"` so per-tenant lookups have a
covering index but the legacy single-column unique constraints on
Name / ClientID / KeyID stay intact for now.

Deferred to a follow-up patch (and called out in the migration's doc
comment): restructuring those Name uniques into composite
(tenant_id, name) so two tenants can independently register a
provider called "openai", an MCP client called "tools", etc. That
restructure has to land alongside coordinated updates to every
`ON CONFLICT (name)` clause in framework/configstore/rdb.go — doing
the index change alone breaks every existing upsert path. Splitting
keeps each commit reviewable.

Until that lands, isolation between tenants is enforced at the
runtime tenant-routing layer (each per-tenant Bifrost runtime sees
only its own scoped slice of provider configs) rather than at the
DB unique index. ClientID / KeyID stay globally unique by design
(FK target and UUID respectively).

Test: TestMigrationAddTenantIDToProviderConfigTables_ColumnAddedAndDefaulted
asserts the column landed on all three tables and the default kicks
in for new inserts. Full framework/configstore + plugins/governance
+ multitenant test suites still pass.

Ref: https://jira.f5net.com/browse/XC-25496
